\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 Setting up for working with jupyter notebooks\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa288\qc\partightenfactor0

\f1\i\b0\fs21 \cf0 Last update: 09/04/2026, Jim Magnuson\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f2\i0 \cf0 It is crucial that you get your computer set up for running Jupyter lab the first week of classes. Because some notebooks require libraries that are not available on Google Colab, the only good solution is for everyone to set up Python and Jupyter on their own computers.\'a0\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 If you do not have a computer you can use for this course, please talk to the instructor ASAP.
\f2\b0 \
Also, if you have more Python experience and are comfortable using conda (or other) environments, see instructions below for Anaconda; for other environments, you will have to set it up on your own (if you get another setup working, it would be great if you could share instructions that I can share with future students!). I'm providing the simplest possible instructions here first.\'a0\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sb240\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 1. Verify that you have Python\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 More than likely, you already have Python on your computer. Your first step is to verify this.\'a0\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sb240\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Macintosh or Linux
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Open a terminal (on a Mac, go to Applications::Utilities and open Terminal.app). Enter this command:\'a0\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 python --version\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 You should see a message like this (though your exact version may be different): 
\f3 Python 3.14.3
\f2 . As long as your version number is 3.4 or greater, you can skip to the 
\f4\i\b Check if you have 
\f5\b0 conda
\f4\b  installed
\f2\i0\b0  section below. If you get a lower version, see link below to install python.\
If instead, you get a message like "
\f3 command not found: python
\f2 ", first try this: \
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 python3 --version
\f2 \
If you still get the command not found message, you need to install Python.\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sb240\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Windows
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Open a Command Prompt or Power Shell: click on the Start Menu. In the search bar, type \'93Command Prompt\'94 and hit Enter. This will open a new Command Prompt Window (enter 'Power Shell' instead if you prefer).\
Enter this command:\'a0\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 python --version\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 You should see a message like this (though your exact version may be different): 
\f3 Python 3.10.9
\f2 . That's great, and you can go to the 
\f4\i\b Check if you have 
\f5\b0 conda
\f4\b  installed
\f2\i0\b0  section below.\'a0\
If instead, you get a message like "
\f3 command not found: python
\f2 ", first try this: \
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 python3 --version
\f2 \
If you still get a message like "
\f3 command not found: python3
\f2 \'94, or if your version is less than 3.4, you need to install Python.\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sb240\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 2. Install Python\'a0-- ONLY if you did not find it in the previous step\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 Go to the official download site and follow the instructions:\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.python.org/downloads/%22%20%5Ct%20%22_blank"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul \ulc2 https://www.python.org/downloads/}}\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sb240\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 3. Check if you have 
\f3\b0 conda
\f0\b  installed\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 We will use 
\f3 conda
\f2  to create a virtual environment where we will install the packages we need for jupyter notebooks. You could install directly in your base environment using pip, but this is not recommended. To see if 
\f3 conda
\f2  is installed, at a command line prompt, enter:\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 conda --version\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 If you get a message like \'93
\f3 conda 24.11.2
\f2 \'94, you can move to the next step. If instead you get a message like \'93
\f3 conda not found
\f2 \'94, you need to install conda. To do so, go to this URL and follow the instructions for your operating system: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.conda.io/projects/conda/en/stable/user-guide/install/index.html"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul \ulc2 https://docs.conda.io/projects/conda/en/stable/user-guide/install/index.html}} \
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sb240\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 4. Installing the packages we need in a virtual environment using 
\f3\b0 conda
\f0\b \
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 First, we will 
\f0\b create a new conda environment
\f2\b0  called 
\f3 jupyter-env
\f2 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 conda create --name jupyter-env python=3.10.9 -c conda-forge\
conda activate jupyter-env\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 Now that jupyter-env is activated, we can install the packages we need. First, tell conda to use the faster \'93libmamba\'94 solver, which also prevents some potential problems with the default solver:\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 conda config --set solver libmamba\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 Now 
\f0\b install the packages
\f2\b0 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 conda install -c conda-forge anywidget ipython ipywidgets "mesa<3" networkx numpy plotly powerlaw matplotlib pandas scipy seaborn scikit-learn statsmodels tk tqdm\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 Note that you will be presented with a much longer list of packages that will be installed. That is because that list includes packages that the ones we want to install depend upon. So answer \'91y\'92 when asked if you want to proceed.\
Let the command run all the way to the end. Downloading the packages is not the last step: once the download bars finish, conda still has to link the files into the environment. The installation is finished only when you see \'93Executing transaction: done\'94 and your command prompt returns. Do not close the terminal before that happens.\
The quotation marks around "mesa<3" are required, and so is the version limit (the \'91<3\'92 part). Our agent-based modeling notebooks use the Mesa 2 interface; it doesn\'92t work with version 3 (yet). \
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Now 
\f0\b check that the packages really installed
\f2\b0 . This is the only reliable way to know. Copy and paste the whole command below as a single line:\

\f3 python -c "import anywidget, IPython, ipywidgets, mesa, networkx, numpy, plotly, powerlaw, matplotlib, pandas, scipy, seaborn, sklearn, statsmodels, tqdm; print('SUCCESS: all packages installed')"\

\f2 If you see SUCCESS: all packages installed, you are done with this step. If instead you see an error like 
\f3 ModuleNotFoundError: No module named \'91seaborn\'92
\f2 , then that package did not install. Install that one package by itself and run the check again, repeating until you get the success message:\

\f3 conda install -c conda-forge seaborn\

\f2 If you ever want to start over with a clean environment, leave the environment, delete it, and go back to the beginning of step 4:\

\f3 conda deactivate\
conda env remove --name jupyter-env
\f2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0
\cf0 If you want to use PyTorch (packages for deep learning, not required for the course notebooks), also do this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 conda install pytorch torchvision -c pytorch\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sb240\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 5. Install and test Jupyter lab
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Now we 
\f0\b install jupyterlab
\f2\b0 . \
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 conda install -c conda-forge jupyterlab\
conda install -c conda-forge ipympl\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 Now try to open jupyter lab with the following command; it should open a new window in your default web browser (Safari, Chrome, Edge, etc.):\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 jupyter lab\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 Of course, if you get errors at this stage or any other, ask me for help. Try to take a screenshot of the error or even better copy and paste the actual text of the error message into your email if possible. Otherwise, the installation steps are done at this point! \
Subsequently, whenever you want to run jupyter lab with this environment, you need to first activate the environment, and then call jupyter lab (you can then navigate to the directory where you have the course notebooks, or you can call Jupyter lab from that directory [much easier]; I\'92ll show you this in class):\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 conda activate jupyter-env\
jupyter lab\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 If you ever forget what you called your environment, you can use this command to list all available environments:\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 conda env list\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sb240\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 6. Get course notebooks\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 Go to the following URL: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/comp-cogneuro-lang/comp-neuro-course"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs22 \cf2 \ul \ulc2 https://github.com/comp-cogneuro-lang/comp-neuro-course}}
\fs22  
\fs21 \
Look for the green Code button. Click it and choose 'Download zip'.\'a0\
Make a directory where you want this to be located and unzip the file.\'a0\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f1\i \cf0 Note: I am improving some of the later notebooks, so they are not yet available. You will need to download this again later in the semester... You can also just download the files you need one at a time. 
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sb240\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 7. Open course notebooks in Jupyter lab\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 At the command line (terminal for Mac or linux,\'a0
\f1\i Command Prompt\'a0
\f2\i0 or\'a0
\f1\i Power Shell\'a0
\f2\i0 for Windows), navigate to the directory/folder where you put the course notebooks. If you don't know how to do that, just skip that step for now. At the command line, enter this command (with a space this time):\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 jupyter lab\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 If you executed this command from the folder where the notebooks are located, you will see a browser as in the screenshot attached to this page. If you could not navigate to the directory location from the command line, just use the jupyter lab interface to navigate there.\'a0
\f0\b However
\f2\b0 , you will want to learn how to navigate to that location -- so please ask if you do not know how.\'a0\
}